# One descriptor, written in the body, lays an accessor on the prototype object of a constructor function: the record of the class expects both halves there (the scan: nameAccessorWrittenInDescriptor()).
# The layout has left: a lookup passes a name that a descriptor defines. What stands above the object the object's own byte says, and that the accessor is the one of the record the guard of the class and the name:
# behind the two the half is run directly, here as a copy in line. The second definition, on a holder that is handed in (written on `Temp.prototype` itself it would leave the record without an expectation), ends the guard, and the accesses go as they are written.
